# The delivery system: what it is and why

{{ project_name }} uses a process for building software with an AI coding agent.
This page explains the idea. `working/README.md` explains what to do.

## The problem

An agent writes code faster than a team can trust it. The failures repeat: the
agent builds something other than what was meant; it makes an assumption and
builds confidently on it; the engineer accepts a recommendation without
understanding what it commits them to; reviewers cannot keep up with larger,
more frequent pull requests; bugs get "fixed" on a plausible cause nobody
verified; asked to find problems, the agent always finds some; and nobody can
say when a piece of work is done.

## The idea

Quality is defined before the work, in writing, and everything after that
checks against the definition.

Before code, a human states what is wanted and why, answers the questions that
would otherwise become assumptions, accepts each decision with its consequence
in front of them, and accepts a specification whose criteria are computed by
tests. After code, machinery confirms the criteria were met and a second human
confirms the specification was the right one.

## Six rules

1. Deterministic before probabilistic. Anything a linter, a test, or a branch
   rule can check is checked that way. An LLM's judgment is never the only
   gate.
2. Human attention is the budget. The human's effort goes to defining the work
   and to a small, defined review. Everything mechanical is removed from their
   path. Review is asked for one thing at a time.
3. Durable state lives in files. The agent's context window is temporary. A
   decision that is not written into `working/` does not exist.
4. The LLM is a tool. Every commit has an engineer as its author. Only an
   engineer can sign an acceptance.
5. Only written standards generate work. A finding must name the rule it
   violates; a concern with no rule is a preference and is not recorded. If the
   team believes something matters, it writes the rule.
6. No silent decisions. Every decision the agent makes is recorded with what it
   considered instead and what happens if it is wrong, and a human accepts it.

## What the engineer does

Three signatures per change (intent, spec, tests), two per bug (intent,
diagnosis), none for a quick change; one review per pull request. The rest is
the agent's work or the machine's. Any engineer can leave the process for a
branch with one signature (`make opt-out`).

## What the system does not claim

It does not make the agent correct; it makes the agent's work checkable. It
does not verify that the intent was the right thing to want; that is the
engineer's judgment, made cheap to exercise and impossible to skip. It enforces
nothing on the local machine that a determined engineer cannot disable; the
trust boundary is the merge, where CI checks artifacts, not tools.

The full design and the reasoning behind each decision are kept with the
template that generated this repository.
